1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,300
In the Dominion of Canada the Province of Ontario at Chalk River the Government

2
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:17,280
of Canada established the first nuclear reactor station outside the United

3
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:27,359
States. And it was here that the famous NRX reactor began operating in 1947. The

4
00:00:27,359 --> 00:00:34,799
The NRX was designed for experimental purposes, to convert normal materials into radioisotopes

5
00:00:34,799 --> 00:00:40,920
and to discover better fuels for more efficient reactors.

6
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:47,359
Mechanical failure, compounded by human error, resulted in a serious power excursion in the

7
00:00:47,359 --> 00:00:55,799
reactor in December of 1952, the first major runaway of an atomic reactor.

8
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:59,980
This is a film record of how the NRX was restored to operation.

9
00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:31,939
There was no prototype for this kind of reactor.

10
00:01:32,579 --> 00:01:37,760
Its unusual vertical configuration allows the shutoff rods to drop by gravity.

11
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:45,680
In order to get a clear idea of what went wrong, where the damage occurred, and what had to be done about it,

12
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:50,300
let's review the main components before the accident.

13
00:01:51,620 --> 00:01:58,180
The diameter of the reactor is 34 feet, and it rises 30 feet above the main floor.

14
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:06,300
This inner tank, or calandria in the reactor, is made of aluminum and holds 3,300 imperial gallons of heavy water.

15
00:02:06,940 --> 00:02:09,300
That's about 18 tons.

16
00:02:10,360 --> 00:02:12,780
Helium gas fills the space above the heavy water.

17
00:02:13,780 --> 00:02:16,920
The tank can accommodate 176 fuel rod assemblies,

18
00:02:17,260 --> 00:02:21,719
each 32 feet long and containing 120 pounds of natural uranium.

19
00:02:22,719 --> 00:02:25,920
Twelve shut-off rods, filled with boron carbide,

20
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:33,900
operate in sequence in banks of 4, 3, 2, 1, 1, 1,

21
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:38,940
and a steel rod filled with slugs of cadmium provides fine control.

22
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:43,440
Below the top cooling water header and master plate

23
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:48,800
are four concrete shields to reduce the radiation to biologically safe levels

24
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:52,960
and three water-cooled shields to remove heat.

25
00:02:52,960 --> 00:03:02,520
Below the Calandria, there are four water-cooled steel shields, and this lead masonite shield.

26
00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:10,580
Surrounding the Calandria is graphite.

27
00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:13,920
This will give you a better view.

28
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:15,300
Here is the Calandria.

29
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:19,200
Nine-inch layer of graphite reflector.

30
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:22,080
Space for thorium or J-rods.

31
00:03:22,080 --> 00:03:24,240
Two more feet of graphite.

32
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:27,520
Two six-inch cast iron shields.

33
00:03:27,520 --> 00:03:29,960
Separated by a cooling air channel.

34
00:03:29,960 --> 00:03:37,860
And finally, seven or eight feet of concrete.

35
00:03:37,860 --> 00:03:42,700
The cooling water from the Ottawa River comes in through the top water header, flows in

36
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:48,060
the aluminum sheath around each uranium rod, and as warm water, goes out through the bottom

37
00:03:48,060 --> 00:03:53,659
header to delay tanks outside the building, where it is held before return to the Ottawa River.

38
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:59,340
Due to an operator's error, three shut-off rods had been raised from the reactor.

39
00:04:00,539 --> 00:04:06,379
Although this condition was corrected, the rods failed to drop into place. However, they did fall

40
00:04:06,379 --> 00:04:11,580
sufficiently to clear the indicator lights on the panel board in the reactor control room upstairs.

41
00:04:12,620 --> 00:04:17,819
Unaware of this condition, an assistant was asked to raise a bank of four rods from the reactor.

42
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:27,199
in carrying out this order the assistant by mistake failed to maintain proper air pressure

43
00:04:27,199 --> 00:04:33,259
at the heads of the shutoff rods with these seven rods out of the core the reactor rose quickly

44
00:04:33,259 --> 00:04:39,379
above critical the assistant scrammed the reactor but since the accelerating air to the shutoff

45
00:04:39,379 --> 00:04:47,220
rods had previously been released only one rod fell slowly into place the power continued to

46
00:04:47,220 --> 00:04:53,960
rise but at a reduced rate. At about 20 megawatts, the rate increased, probably because cooling

47
00:04:53,960 --> 00:05:00,540
water had then boiled out of some rod channels. The heavy water was then dumped from the calandria.

48
00:05:01,260 --> 00:05:09,160
The power level fell after reaching 80 megawatts. In the excursion, cladding of a number of

49
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:16,480
fuel rods as well as some uranium melted. Outer sheaths burst and a number of calandria

50
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:23,100
tubes failed. At least one of the fuel rods blew apart and uranium fused together with

51
00:05:23,100 --> 00:05:36,560
aluminum in some tubes of the Calandria. Cooling water, air, heavy water, helium gas, and intensely

52
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:43,040
radioactive fuel rod material mixed together and gushed into the basement. The NRX was

53
00:05:43,040 --> 00:05:48,300
not designed to provide for repairs in the event of major damage. Moreover, restoration would have

54
00:05:48,300 --> 00:05:53,680
been much simpler without the presence of so much radioactivity. There were many grams of fission

55
00:05:53,680 --> 00:05:59,040
products in the metal and oxide in the reactor. Months later, this material still indicated an

56
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:04,600
activity of one curie per gram. Restoration of the NRX was started under these conditions.

57
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:10,939
Cooling water had to be kept on highly irradiated rods in the reactor. Immediate consideration had

58
00:06:10,939 --> 00:06:15,779
to be given to the 100,000 gallons of water collecting in the basement. Since it was highly

59
00:06:15,779 --> 00:06:21,339
contaminated with long-lived fish and products, it was pumped into holding tanks. During the period

60
00:06:21,339 --> 00:06:26,740
when continued cooling was required, about 1 million gallons of contaminated water containing

61
00:06:26,740 --> 00:06:34,259
10,000 curies of fish and products had to be dealt with. With storage tanks at capacity,

62
00:06:34,959 --> 00:06:40,240
this pipeline had to be built in zero weather to carry water to a disposal area.

63
00:06:40,939 --> 00:06:49,939
Here it seeped away through a mixture of sand and clay to a small creek where tests showed no radioactivity.

64
00:06:49,939 --> 00:06:57,939
General contamination was present during removal of the steel cover plate, top water header, master plate,

65
00:06:57,939 --> 00:07:03,939
four biological concrete shields, and the topmost steel shield.

66
00:07:03,939 --> 00:07:11,939
The underside of the second steel thermal shield measured four Roentgens per hour in contact.

67
00:07:11,939 --> 00:07:18,939
We had planned to use the third steel shield as protection for the workman during removal of damaged rod from the calandria.

68
00:07:18,939 --> 00:07:23,939
It proved to be difficult to cut holes through this steel shield.

69
00:07:23,939 --> 00:07:31,939
So it was removed, covered with a tarpaulin and buried at the disposal area.

70
00:07:31,939 --> 00:07:38,939
Since the calandria had to be replaced, different methods of removing the damaged fuel rods were tried.

71
00:07:46,939 --> 00:07:54,939
Using this temporary lead shield as a protection for the workmen, we trepanned the top tube sheet.

72
00:07:54,939 --> 00:08:06,620
With this cutting tool and tube clamping device, the plan was to cut the calandria tube three

73
00:08:06,620 --> 00:08:09,139
inches from the bottom tube sheet.

74
00:08:09,139 --> 00:08:13,079
This was to prevent the highly irradiated material in the bottom of the calandria from

75
00:08:13,079 --> 00:08:16,939
spilling into the basement.

76
00:08:16,939 --> 00:08:19,600
It didn't work out as planned.

77
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:24,759
The uranium fuel rod and aluminum calandria tube had fused together.

78
00:08:24,759 --> 00:08:29,219
cutting tool had not cut through the fuel rod, and this device pulled the fused rod

79
00:08:29,219 --> 00:08:34,819
and calandria tube forcibly from the bottom tube sheet. Contamination spilled into the

80
00:08:34,819 --> 00:08:43,819
basement. When that happened, this clamping tool was designed to pull the remainder of

81
00:08:43,819 --> 00:08:48,939
the damaged rods and tubes bodily from the bottom tube sheet. Fish and products falling

82
00:08:48,940 --> 00:08:51,460
into the basement were no longer a consideration.

83
00:08:55,260 --> 00:09:01,360
And with this arrangement, we snared a number of active pieces of uranium lying on the bottom

84
00:09:01,360 --> 00:09:01,940
tube sheet.

85
00:09:04,780 --> 00:09:09,960
Damaged rods and active pieces were pulled from the calandria into this flask for shielding

86
00:09:09,960 --> 00:09:10,680
before disposal.

87
00:09:11,600 --> 00:09:16,080
The bottom water header had been removed, and after the damaged rods were taken out

88
00:09:16,080 --> 00:09:20,820
of the calandria, the lead masonite shield was lowered and removed through the basement.

89
00:09:21,740 --> 00:09:25,820
Supporting rods were attached to the shield up through the calandria where the overhead

90
00:09:25,820 --> 00:09:33,320
crane lowered it. A high-pressure water hose was then used to flush the calandria of all

91
00:09:33,320 --> 00:09:37,800
the bits and pieces which could not be snared. These went into a tray located in the space

92
00:09:37,800 --> 00:09:45,060
below. To remove the calandria, 70 men were briefed with a scale model in

93
00:09:45,060 --> 00:09:49,260
plywood. The procedure was rehearsed several times until each staff member

94
00:09:49,260 --> 00:10:00,180
knew exactly what he had to do. Engineers were not satisfied that the training

95
00:10:00,180 --> 00:10:04,840
with the plywood model was sufficient. A steel dummy of the same diameter was

96
00:10:04,840 --> 00:10:09,660
made and filled with sand to the gross weight of the calandria. The dummy run

97
00:10:09,660 --> 00:10:14,860
was made the night before removing the actual calandria. A practice run will

98
00:10:14,860 --> 00:10:19,080
turn up errors such as these workmen standing near the dummy. This type of

99
00:10:19,080 --> 00:10:23,019
training is recommended in a hazardous undertaking such as this where the real

100
00:10:23,019 --> 00:10:33,560
calandria measured 100 renkins to 300 renkins per hour in contact. The actual

101
00:10:33,560 --> 00:10:37,560
operation began the next day. An announcer in the reactor control room

102
00:10:37,560 --> 00:10:42,760
called out each of the 28 steps involved in the removal. The lifting jig was

103
00:10:42,760 --> 00:10:47,400
lowered. Dogs attached to the top tube sheet at the calandria and the stained

104
00:10:47,400 --> 00:10:52,100
tank rose above the reactor structure. The lift was halted for four minutes to

105
00:10:52,100 --> 00:10:56,300
take photographs and radiation measurements. We wanted particularly to

106
00:10:56,300 --> 00:11:00,200
find out the condition of the expansion joint in the calandria. Photos showed it

107
00:11:00,200 --> 00:11:06,000
be in good condition. The crane operator was about 75 feet away protected by a shielded cab.

108
00:11:06,440 --> 00:11:12,440
He could not see the calandria and had to be guided by signals. He resumed the hoisting and

109
00:11:12,440 --> 00:11:18,040
easily managed the radioactive calandria through the guide ring and onto the skid below just as

110
00:11:18,039 --> 00:11:29,799
had handled the dummy the evening before.

111
00:11:29,799 --> 00:11:35,099
Workmen shielded behind blocks of concrete, pulled draw ropes to enclose the calandria

112
00:11:35,099 --> 00:11:38,599
and decrease the spread of radioactivity.

113
00:11:38,599 --> 00:11:42,360
The yoke was lowered to back the crane hook off the attachment to the lifting jig, then

114
00:11:42,360 --> 00:11:45,899
the calandria was lowered to the horizontal position.

115
00:11:45,899 --> 00:11:51,480
The skid supporting the calandria was attached to a road grater, equipped with a lead shield to protect the operator.

116
00:11:52,879 --> 00:11:57,679
A long blast of the plant whistle warned men on duty that the calandria was being towed out through the plant.

117
00:11:58,240 --> 00:12:03,340
As this procession went by these buildings, continuous radiation measuring instruments went completely off scale.

118
00:12:03,759 --> 00:12:09,899
The usually well-guarded main gate was strangely unattended and wide open as the calandria moved to the disposal area.

119
00:12:09,899 --> 00:12:17,279
The calandria was placed in a slot in the sand and covered completely except for one end facing

120
00:12:17,279 --> 00:12:23,459
the woods. This end was left exposed so we could later take samples of the aluminum tube sheet for

121
00:12:23,459 --> 00:12:31,620
study and research. Some water remained in the calandria when it was removed. On the way to the

122
00:12:31,620 --> 00:12:38,159
disposal area, some of this leaked out through the tarpaulin around the calandria. These workmen

123
00:12:38,159 --> 00:12:44,879
scouring flushing and monitoring the road to make sure all the radioactive contamination is removed

124
00:12:48,799 --> 00:12:52,319
laundering became a major problem during the dismantling of the reactor

125
00:12:53,120 --> 00:12:56,879
every item of apparel had to be completely sorted cleaned and checked

126
00:12:57,439 --> 00:13:04,480
for residual contamination before it was returned to service clothing costs in the first five days

127
00:13:04,480 --> 00:13:08,960
after the accident went up by a factor of 20 times.

128
00:13:08,960 --> 00:13:12,879
Later these costs were reduced to about twice the usual level

129
00:13:12,879 --> 00:13:16,800
by adopting better methods of handling.

130
00:13:19,039 --> 00:13:23,039
Throughout the dismantling of the reactor each piece was decontaminated

131
00:13:23,039 --> 00:13:27,519
after it was removed. A large concrete pit adjacent to the reactor called the

132
00:13:27,519 --> 00:13:30,800
swimming pool was used to decontaminate large pieces

133
00:13:30,800 --> 00:13:34,159
of equipment. A temporary shelter with a removable top

134
00:13:34,159 --> 00:13:39,620
over the pit allowed these large pieces to be handled by crane. This is the master plate

135
00:13:39,620 --> 00:13:47,299
coming up from the swimming pool. Heavy water which had been dumped into the storage tank,

136
00:13:48,019 --> 00:13:52,799
including some that had overflowed into the helium gas holder during the accident, was

137
00:13:52,799 --> 00:14:03,079
removed and decontaminated by the use of ion exchange resins. The heavy water cooler, pump,

138
00:14:04,159 --> 00:14:12,219
storage tanks were also removed and decontaminated. The concrete floor and

139
00:14:12,219 --> 00:14:16,480
walls under the reactor were heavily contaminated. Whenever possible this

140
00:14:16,480 --> 00:14:21,659
concrete was flame primed before chipping, grinding, and sandblasting. It

141
00:14:21,659 --> 00:14:26,079
was found that contamination was much easier to remove when the concrete was

142
00:14:26,079 --> 00:14:32,100
dry rather than wet. Each device has a vacuum attachment to carry off the dust

143
00:14:32,100 --> 00:14:38,700
and grit, and workmen are still wearing respirators. Generally, removing a half inch of concrete

144
00:14:38,700 --> 00:14:43,659
eliminated the radioactivity. However, in the basement where activity remained after

145
00:14:43,659 --> 00:14:50,420
sandblasting, a six-inch layer of concrete was placed on the floor and walls. A vinyl-based

146
00:14:50,420 --> 00:14:55,100
paint was sprayed to fix low levels of activity and to cover new concrete surfaces which were

147
00:14:55,100 --> 00:14:59,600
left exposed. This paint forms a glossy surface which is easily decontaminated.

148
00:15:02,100 --> 00:15:13,100
We found it easier to decontaminate the lead from the walls and floor in the lower header room by melting it down, skimming it, and remolding new sections.

149
00:15:13,100 --> 00:15:24,100
In addition, the walls and floor of the new location for the heavy water storage tanks and coolers were re-lined with lead in case of any future spill of heavy water.

150
00:15:24,100 --> 00:15:33,100
A number of undamaged fuel rods had been irradiated from 1,000 to 3,000 megawatt days per ton.

151
00:15:33,100 --> 00:15:38,220
This is the method we used to preserve these rods during the 14-month period required to

152
00:15:38,220 --> 00:15:40,300
restore the reactor.

153
00:15:40,300 --> 00:15:45,139
If these rods had been exposed to stagnant water, they would have corroded and be of

154
00:15:45,139 --> 00:15:48,320
no further use in the reactor.

155
00:15:48,320 --> 00:15:52,899
Ruptured rods were stored in a trench system after removal from the pile.

156
00:15:52,899 --> 00:15:58,840
Here they are being photographed for detailed study later.

157
00:15:58,840 --> 00:16:03,459
This is a photograph made with the use of a pinhole camera.

158
00:16:03,459 --> 00:16:08,919
The negative is also a pinhole photograph made with gamma rays streaming from radioactive

159
00:16:08,919 --> 00:16:10,419
bits.

160
00:16:10,419 --> 00:16:17,179
By superimposing the negative over the photograph, radioactive material is readily located at

161
00:16:17,179 --> 00:16:22,340
these two points in the piping leading to the pumps.

162
00:16:22,340 --> 00:16:26,300
The helium gas holder had been decontaminated and put back in place.

163
00:16:26,300 --> 00:16:32,080
Now the heavy water coolers and the heavy water storage tank were similarly installed

164
00:16:32,080 --> 00:16:38,280
in the location, which was equipped with lead walls and floor.

165
00:16:38,280 --> 00:16:42,840
Radiation in contact with the first thermal shield below the calandria was 500 roentgens

166
00:16:42,840 --> 00:16:43,840
per hour.

167
00:16:43,840 --> 00:16:49,740
To permit NRX to operate at 40 megawatts compared to 30 megawatts before the accident, a new

168
00:16:49,740 --> 00:16:54,940
stainless steel water-cooled sandwich was built to go over the first thermal shield below the

169
00:16:54,940 --> 00:17:00,860
calandria it was designed to carry away excess heat when operating the reactor at the higher power

170
00:17:02,620 --> 00:17:06,220
this shield is being checked for alignment with the new calandria before installation

171
00:17:11,019 --> 00:17:21,880
the first job on the new calandria was to check it for leaks Helium at seven pounds pressure was applied to the vessel, and a helium leak detector

172
00:17:21,880 --> 00:17:30,620
is used around the rolled joints in the tube sheets.

173
00:17:30,620 --> 00:17:33,960
Three tubes had to be re-rolled several times.

174
00:17:33,960 --> 00:17:43,140
As a result, these tubes became so thin that they had to be replaced.

175
00:17:43,140 --> 00:17:48,080
In pulling the tubes, these black marks were the first indication that graphite had been

176
00:17:48,080 --> 00:17:51,680
used in the fabrication of the calandria.

177
00:17:51,680 --> 00:17:56,840
We had learned many years before that aluminum in contact with graphite will set up an electrolytic

178
00:17:56,840 --> 00:17:57,840
reaction.

179
00:17:57,840 --> 00:18:06,700
If this graphite were allowed to remain, the calandria would have a limited life.

180
00:18:06,700 --> 00:18:15,460
This last-minute discovery caused a major delay in the program.

181
00:18:15,460 --> 00:18:19,759
The calandria was moved from the reactor building to shops, where the men are pulling the tubes

182
00:18:19,759 --> 00:18:30,299
without wearing respirators.

183
00:18:30,299 --> 00:18:35,460
A hole was cut in the calandria shell, in order to clean out every particle of graphite

184
00:18:35,460 --> 00:18:36,579
which might be inside.

185
00:18:38,079 --> 00:18:41,680
We found that vigorous wiping with cloths was superior to vacuuming.

186
00:18:45,340 --> 00:18:48,299
New tubes were received within two weeks of placing the order.

187
00:18:52,819 --> 00:18:56,059
They were put into the calandria with the use of shim stock material

188
00:18:56,059 --> 00:18:59,100
to prevent galling between the tubes and the tube sheets.

189
00:19:01,620 --> 00:19:05,200
Plugs for gas and water passages were completely cleaned

190
00:19:05,200 --> 00:19:10,440
and screwed into the tube sheets.

191
00:19:10,440 --> 00:19:14,100
Then the vessel was completely checked to assure that there were no leaks

192
00:19:14,100 --> 00:19:19,480
before it was transferred to the reactor building.

193
00:19:19,480 --> 00:19:26,480
The calandria is now in place and this man is installing the alignment bracket on it.

194
00:19:27,400 --> 00:19:34,240
The radiation coming up around this circle was 20 R per hour.

195
00:19:34,240 --> 00:19:38,960
A new aluminum shield had been obtained to replace the third steel shield, which was previously buried.

196
00:19:39,500 --> 00:19:44,819
Four holes in this shield were out of alignment with the calandria tubes as a result of welding stresses

197
00:19:44,819 --> 00:19:47,660
and had to be re-drilled to center them properly.

198
00:19:48,640 --> 00:19:56,640
The half-life of irradiated aluminum is very much shorter than that of iron-59 and cobalt-60 formed in steel when irradiated.

199
00:19:57,160 --> 00:20:00,180
Hence, the aluminum shield can be removed much more quickly and safely.

200
00:20:04,240 --> 00:20:07,420
The new aluminum shield was put into the reactor.

201
00:20:07,420 --> 00:20:09,420
A steel shield followed.

202
00:20:09,420 --> 00:20:13,000
Now the topmost thermal shield is being moved for installation.

203
00:20:23,680 --> 00:20:27,319
Gas and water connections are being made above this shield.

204
00:20:27,319 --> 00:20:30,220
Notice that the men are no longer wearing respirators.

205
00:20:34,240 --> 00:20:40,900
Meanwhile, in the basement, these pressure switches, which had been individually decontaminated,

206
00:20:40,900 --> 00:20:44,480
are being reinstalled.

207
00:20:44,480 --> 00:20:50,839
During the accident, the contaminated water rose halfway up these panel boards.

208
00:20:50,839 --> 00:20:55,480
The four concrete biological shields were returned to position, and followed by the

209
00:20:55,480 --> 00:20:57,420
master plate.

210
00:20:57,420 --> 00:21:03,220
Top and bottom water headers, control rod, shell off rods, and fuel rods were put back

211
00:21:03,220 --> 00:21:08,100
into place and the reactor was started again.

212
00:21:15,720 --> 00:21:23,079
During the restoration the safety system was carefully reviewed. Today NRX is

213
00:21:23,079 --> 00:21:27,460
equipped with two independent means of shutting down the reactor. There are now

214
00:21:27,460 --> 00:21:32,860
six individually operated shut-off rods of improved design. They cannot be raised

215
00:21:32,860 --> 00:21:35,860
faster than one in thirty seconds.

216
00:21:35,860 --> 00:21:38,860
Moreover, when the reactor is down to change fuel rods,

217
00:21:38,860 --> 00:21:41,860
four of the shutoff rods are kept in an elevated position

218
00:21:41,860 --> 00:21:43,860
as a safety bank.

219
00:21:44,860 --> 00:21:47,860
These rods fall into the reactor if an unplanned increase

220
00:21:47,860 --> 00:21:50,860
in reactivity occurs during shutdown.

221
00:21:50,860 --> 00:21:53,860
A second change allows heavy water to be dumped

222
00:21:53,860 --> 00:21:58,860
automatically on the scram and at a much increased dump rate.

223
00:21:58,860 --> 00:22:04,000
Triplicated scram channels are used.

224
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:08,920
To reduce unnecessary shutdown, coincidence signals are required on a channel to scram

225
00:22:08,920 --> 00:22:10,780
the reactor.

226
00:22:10,780 --> 00:22:15,420
This permits servicing a single instrument while the reactor is operating with no loss

227
00:22:15,420 --> 00:22:17,420
in safety.

228
00:22:17,420 --> 00:22:23,520
The runaway caused a major disruption in the work of Chalk River.

229
00:22:23,519 --> 00:22:28,660
Mechanically, a few uranium rods melted and damaged the calandria tubes.

230
00:22:29,700 --> 00:22:35,819
Radioactive contamination prolonged the repair enormously, but at no time was out of control.

231
00:22:36,779 --> 00:22:40,059
No case of harmful overexposure resulted.
